unless you can reproduce the problem yourself.


Ground rules:
- The C++ library header (include/indicators.hpp) does NOT exist in this repo. The only reference
  for library behaviour is the compiled binary /workspace/dataset/test2_executable (objdump/nm/gdb are available) and
  the caller /workspace/dataset/test2.cpp.
- Do NOT modify anything under /output unless your task explicitly says to. Use /tmp for scratch.
- If you build the Rust project with cargo, set CARGO_TARGET_DIR to a unique /tmp dir so parallel
  agents don't contend on the same target lock. rustc is 1.75.0; g++ 12 is at /opt/compiler/gcc-12/bin/g++.
- Zero external crates are allowed: std only. Rust edition 2021.
- Report facts you actually observed by running commands. Never claim a test passed without output.


Deliverable under review: /output (Rust port of /workspace/dataset/test2.cpp; reference binary /workspace/dataset/test2_executable).
Original C++ semantics:

indicators::ProgressBar — memory layout recovered from the ctor at 0x402790:
  @0x00 int progress          default 0
  @0x04 int total             default 100
  @0x08 int width             default 50   (movl $0x32)
  @0x0c bool show_percentage  default true
  @0x0d bool show_bar         default true   (gates opening glyph + body + closing glyph)
  @0x10 std::string fill      "="
  @0x18 std::string lead      ">"
  @0x20 std::string remainder " "
  @0x28 std::string start     "["
  @0x30 std::string end       "]"

std::string ProgressBar::str() const  — recovered from 0x402b20:
  std::ostringstream os;
  if (show_bar) {                       // cmpb 0xd(%rbx)
    os << start;
    int completed = (int)((float)progress / (float)total * (float)width);
        // cvtsi2ss progress; cvtsi2ss total; divss; cvtsi2ss width; mulss; cvttss2si
    for (int i = 0; i < width; ++i) {   // loop skipped entirely when width <= 0
      if (completed > i)       os << fill;
      else if (completed == i) os << lead;
      else                     os << remainder;
    }
    os << end;
  }
  if (show_percentage) {                // cmpb 0xc(%rbx)
    if (show_bar) os << " ";
    os << std::fixed << std::setprecision(1)
       << ((double)((float)progress / (float)total) * 100.0) << "%";
       // divss -> cvtss2sd -> mulsd 100.0 -> _M_insert<double>; precision=1, fixed flag 0x4
  }
  return os.str();

indicators::Percentage(float value): str() == fixed/setprecision(1) of ((double)value * 100.0), then "%".
  (In main the value is constant-folded to the double 75.0 at 0x403070.)

main() takes argc/argv and never reads them: set_progress(75); set_total(100); set_width(30);
cout << bar.str() << endl; Percentage pct(0.75f); cout << pct.str() << endl; return 0;
Program output is therefore invariant across all command lines:
  "[======================>       ] 75.0%\n75.0%\n"


Reported finding (from the "requirements-audit" pass):
  title: Rust binary panics on non-UTF-8 argv; C++ binary ignores argv entirely (requirement 2 violated)
  file: /output/test2.rs
  severity: major
  detail: Line 16 is `let _args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();`. `std::env::args()` is documented to panic if any argument is not valid Unicode, and it does: the process aborts with exit code 101 and prints nothing to stdout. The C++ main() provably never reads argc/argv (the disassembly of <main> at 0x402240 pushes r13/r12/rbp/rbx, subtracts from rsp and goes straight to `lea 0x20(%rsp),%rdi; call ProgressBar::ProgressBar()` -- %edi and %rsi are clobbered without ever being read), so the C++ prints the normal two lines and exits 0 for literally any argv, including non-UTF-8 bytes. Requirement 2 explicitly demands the Rust side "never errors, never changes output, never panics" for any argv, so this is a provable divergence. It is reachable in practice: a filename with invalid UTF-8 bytes, a latin-1 locale, or any harness that passes raw bytes triggers it. The line has no purpose other than to mirror `int main(int argc, char* argv[])` -- `_args` is never used -- so the panic is pure downside. Note the checked-in /output/test2 binary has the same defect (exit 101 on 0xFF argv).
  evidence: $ /tmp/ra/final $'\xff'
thread 'main' panicked at library/std/src/env.rs:807:51:
called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: "\xFF"
note: run with RUST_BACKTRACE=1 environment variable to display a backtrace
rc=101 stdout_bytes=0

$ /workspace/dataset/test2_executable $'\xff' >/dev/null 2>&1; echo cpp rc=$?
cpp rc=0

$ /workspace/dataset/test2_executable $'\xff\xfe' $'bad\x80arg' | od -c
0000000   [   =   =   =   =   =   =   =   =   =   =   =   =   =   =   =
0000020   =   =   =   =   =   =   =   >                               ]
0000040       7   5   .   0   %  \n   7   5   .   0   %  \n
0000055
cpp exit=0

(all UTF-8 argv cases do match:)
args=[] rc=0 stderr_bytes=0 match=YES
args=[--progress 5 --total 7 --width 3] rc=0 stderr_bytes=0 match=YES
args=[--help] rc=0 stderr_bytes=0 match=YES
args=[-x --unknown=1] rc=0 stderr_bytes=0 match=YES
  suggested fix: Replace line 16 with the OsString form, which cannot panic:

    // NOTE: `std::env::args()` panics on non-UTF-8 argv; the C++ never does.
    let _args: Vec<std::ffi::OsString> = std::env::args_os().collect();

(Or simply delete the line -- the C++ never reads argv.) I applied exactly this edit to a copy at /tmp/ra/fix/test2.rs and verified it: `rustc -O --edition 2021` exit 0 and `rustc -O` (edition 2015) exit 0, both warning-free; `cmp <(/tmp/ra/fix/t $'\xff') <(/workspace/dataset/test2_executable)` -> CMP-OK-fixed-nonutf8; `cmp <(/tmp/ra/fix/t) <(...)` -> CMP-OK-fixed-noargs; argc=0 via execve -> exit 0.

Judge it through the correctness lens — is the described behaviour actually wrong relative to the C++ binary/spec? Reproduce it by running commands.

Reproduce or refute by running commands. Do NOT modify /output. Then answer: is this real and worth
fixing? If real, state the minimal correct fix precisely (exact file, exact code change).

