input.minimal_fix:
File: /output/test2.rs — replace line 16 (the only change needed; also rebuild the checked-in /output/test2, which carries the same defect).

Exact replacement of:

    let _args: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();

with:

    let _args: Vec<std::ffi::OsString> = std::env::args_os().collect();

Optionally extend the existing comment on lines 14-15 to record why, e.g.:

    // `int main(int argc, char* argv[])` accepts arguments but never reads
    // them, so the output is the same for any command line. Use `args_os`:
    // `std::env::args()` panics on non-UTF-8 argv, which the C++ never does.
    let _args: Vec<std::ffi::OsString> = std::env::args_os().collect();

Why this is minimal and safe: `std::env::args_os()` yields `OsString` directly and performs no UTF-8 validation, so it cannot panic. `std::ffi::OsString` is written as a fully-qualified path, so no `use` statement is added and no unused-import warning appears. `_args` keeps its leading underscore, so no unused-variable warning. Nothing else in main reads `_args`, so no downstream code changes.

Deleting line 16 outright is equally correct (the C++ never reads argv) and I verified it builds warning-free and matches the C++; I recommend the `args_os` form only because it preserves the author's evident intent of mirroring the C++ `int main(int argc, char* argv[])` signature.

Verified after the edit on a scratch copy: warning-free builds under `rustc -O test2.rs` (edition 2015), `rustc -O --edition 2021 test2.rs`, and `cargo build --release`; stdout matches /workspace/dataset/test2_executable byte-for-byte via `cmp` on all 8 tested argv shapes (including 6 non-UTF-8) and on argc=0, with exit code 0 and empty stderr throughout.
